# Template for projects/open-notebook/secrets.enc.env, which is committed
# *encrypted*. Create it with:
#   make secrets FILE=projects/open-notebook/secrets.enc.env
# The deploy workflow decrypts it to /srv/open-notebook/.env at 0600 before
# `docker compose up`. Compose reads that file twice: as `env_file` for the app
# container, and for the ${...} references in compose.yml.
#
# Compose interpolates `${VAR}` in this file before the container sees it, so a
# literal `$` in a value must be doubled to `$$`. The `openssl rand -base64`
# values suggested below never contain one.
#
# Provider API keys are deliberately absent. Open Notebook v1 keeps them in its
# own database, encrypted with OPEN_NOTEBOOK_ENCRYPTION_KEY, and you add them in
# the UI at Settings -> API Keys; the environment-variable path is deprecated
# upstream and does not support per-service base URLs (which the OpenCode Zen
# credential needs).

# Gates the entire instance, and is also the API's bearer token. Unset, Open
# Notebook serves everything to anyone who finds the hostname — compose.yml
# refuses to start without it. Something long, from a password manager.
OPEN_NOTEBOOK_PASSWORD=

# Encrypts the provider credentials (OpenCode Zen, OpenRouter, ElevenLabs) at
# rest in SurrealDB. 16+ characters:  openssl rand -base64 32
#
# It lives in .env rather than ./data, so the nightly backup never contains it —
# which is what keeps a stolen backup useless, and also means a restore without
# this value cannot decrypt the stored keys. There is no rotation path: changing
# it orphans every credential and they must be re-entered.
OPEN_NOTEBOOK_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# SurrealDB's root password, passed to both the server and the app.
#   openssl rand -base64 24
# The database publishes no port and shares a private network with nothing but
# the app, so this is depth rather than the perimeter — but upstream's root:root
# default is one stray `ports:` line away from mattering.
SURREAL_PASSWORD=

# https://firecrawl.dev — scrapes URLs that plain HTML extraction cannot:
# JavaScript-rendered pages and anti-bot-protected sites. Unlike the model and
# voice providers, this one is *not* a Settings -> API Keys credential: it is
# read from the environment, so it belongs here and nowhere else.
#
# Its presence alone changes behaviour. The default `auto` URL engine tries
# Firecrawl -> Jina -> Crawl4AI -> simple and skips whatever is unconfigured, so
# a key here silently promotes Firecrawl to first choice; Settings -> Content
# Processing can pin it instead. Leave it blank and extraction falls back to
# bs4, which is fine for static pages and empty-handed on the rest.
#
# Optional tuning, same mechanism, both passed through to content-core:
#   CCORE_FIRECRAWL_PROXY      basic | stealth | auto (default)
#   CCORE_FIRECRAWL_WAIT_FOR   ms to wait for JS to render, default 3000
# FIRECRAWL_API_URL points at a self-hosted instance instead of the service.
FIRECRAWL_API_KEY=
